L000200	\appendix
L000201	\section{Entanglement Spectrum and Eigenvalues of the Correlation Matrix}
L000202	In this section, we provide a brief derivation of why the eigenvalues of the correlation matrix correspond to the entanglement spectrum for noninteracting systems; comprehensive derivations can be found in several references \cite{peschel2009reduced,chang2020entanglement}. The correlation matrix is defined as
L000203	
L000204	\begin{equation}
L000205	C_{\alpha\beta} \equiv \operatorname{Tr}(\rho_A \phi_{\alpha}^\dagger \phi_{\beta}) = \langle G | \phi_{\alpha}^\dagger \phi_\beta | G \rangle,
L000206	\label{eq:s1}
L000207	\end{equation}
L000208	where $| G \rangle$ denotes the ground state, and $\alpha$, $\beta$ label the positions of lattice sites. The ground state can be expressed as
L000209	\begin{equation}
L000210	| G \rangle = \prod_{n \in \text{occ}} \left( \sum_{\alpha} \psi_{n,\alpha} \phi_{\alpha} \right)^\dagger | 0 \rangle,
L000211	\label{eq:s2}
L000212	\end{equation}
L000213	where $| 0 \rangle$ is the vacuum state, and $H | \psi_n \rangle = \epsilon_n | \psi_n \rangle$ with $| \psi_n \rangle$ denoting the $n$th eigenstate of the Hamiltonian $H$. Using this expression for the ground state together with the commutation relation $\{\phi_{\alpha}^\dagger , \phi_{\beta}\}=\delta_{\alpha\beta}$ and the condition $\phi_{\alpha}| 0\rangle = 0$, the correlation matrix simplifies to
L000214	\begin{equation}
L000215	C_{\alpha\beta} = \sum_{n \in \text{occ}} \psi_{n,\alpha} \psi_{n,\beta}^*,
L000216	\label{eq:s3}
L000217	\end{equation}
L000218	where $\psi_{n,\beta}^*$ denotes the complex conjugate of $\psi_{n,\beta}$.
L000219	
L000220	The correlation matrix $C$ and the entanglement Hamiltonian $H^E$ appearing in the reduced density matrix $\rho_A \propto \exp\left(-\sum_{\alpha\beta} \phi_{\alpha}^\dagger H_{\alpha\beta}^{E} \phi_\beta\right),$ can be simultaneously diagonalized, leading to the relation
L000221	\begin{equation}
L000222	C = \frac{e^{-H^E}}{1 + e^{-H^E}}.
L000223	\label{eq:s4}
L000224	\end{equation}
L000225	Consequently, the entanglement entropy for subsystem $A$ is given by
L000226	\begin{equation}
L000227	S_A = - \sum_{\delta} \left[ \varepsilon_\delta \ln \varepsilon_\delta + (1 - \varepsilon_\delta) \ln (1 - \varepsilon_\delta) \right],
L000228	\label{eq:s5}
L000229	\end{equation}
L000230	where $\varepsilon_\delta$ are the eigenvalues of $C$. Hence, the eigenvalues of the correlation matrix directly encode the entanglement spectrum.
