L000233	\section{Gapless nature for odd number of layers}
L000234	
L000235	In this section, we demonstrate that the model considered in the main text becomes \textbf{gapless} when the number of layers $N$ is odd.
L000236	
L000237	Consider a stack of $N$ layers of a trivial subsystem, each possessing chiral symmetry. The momentum-space Hamiltonian takes the block-tridiagonal form
L000238	\begin{equation}
L000239	H(k) =
L000240	\begin{pmatrix}
L000241	H_{\mathrm{onsite}}(k) & T      & 0      & \cdots & 0      \\
L000242	T^{\dagger}            & H_{\mathrm{onsite}}(k) & T      & \cdots & 0      \\
L000243	0                      & T^{\dagger} & H_{\mathrm{onsite}}(k) & \ddots & \vdots \\
L000244	\vdots                 & \vdots & \ddots & \ddots & T      \\
L000245	0                      & 0      & \cdots & T^{\dagger} & H_{\mathrm{onsite}}(k)
L000246	\end{pmatrix},
L000247	\label{eq:Hk_full}
L000248	\end{equation}
L000249	where the intralayer Hamiltonian is
L000250	\begin{equation}
L000251	H_{\mathrm{onsite}}(k) = t\,(1+\cos k)\,\sigma_x + t\sin k\,\sigma_y,
L000252	\label{eq:H_onsite}
L000253	\end{equation}
L000254	and the interlayer hopping matrix is
L000255	\begin{equation}
L000256	T \equiv t_1 \sigma_x.
L000257	\label{eq:T_matrix}
L000258	\end{equation}
L000259	
L000260	At the time-reversal invariant momenta $k = \pm\pi$, we have $H_{\mathrm{onsite}}(\pm\pi)=0$, and the Hamiltonian reduces to
L000261	\begin{equation}
L000262	H(\pm\pi) =
L000263	\begin{pmatrix}
L000264	0 & T      & 0      & \cdots & 0      \\
L000265	T^{\dagger} & 0      & T      & \cdots & 0      \\
L000266	0 & T^{\dagger} & 0      & \ddots & \vdots \\
L000267	\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \ddots & T      \\
L000268	0 & 0      & \cdots & T^{\dagger} & 0
L000269	\end{pmatrix}.
L000270	\label{eq:H_pi}
L000271	\end{equation}
L000272	
L000273	Direct inspection shows that $H(\pm\pi)$ possesses zero-energy eigenstates of the form
L000274	\begin{equation}
L000275	\Psi(\pm\pi) = \bigl[ \psi_1,\;0,\;\psi_3,\;0,\;\dots,\;0 \bigr]^{\!\mathsf{T}},
L000276	\qquad \psi_1 = -\psi_3,
L000277	\label{eq:zero_mode}
L000278	\end{equation}
L000279	where the superscript $\mathsf{T}$ denotes the transpose.
L000280	
L000281	Because the system respects chiral symmetry, the zero modes at $\pm\pi$ must appear in even numbers. Combined with the continuity of the band structure in $k$-space, this implies that for \textbf{odd} $N$, the bulk spectrum remains gapless.
L000282	
L000283	Together with the entanglement-spectrum analysis presented in the main text, this result confirms the existence of topological bound states in the continuum (BICs) in odd-layer systems.
